The Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) is a networking protocol used on IPv4 networks to find the MAC (physical) address of a device when you already know its IP address.

Think of it like this:

IP address = A person's mailing address (where they live)
MAC address = Their actual name on the mailbox (who receives the mail)

A computer needs the MAC address because Ethernet networks deliver data based on MAC addresses, not IP addresses.

# Where ARP Fits
| Step        | Uses                              |
| ----------- | --------------------------------- |
| Application | Website or program                |
| TCP/UDP     | Transport                         |
| IP          | Finds the destination network     |
| **ARP**     | Finds the destination MAC address |
| Ethernet    | Sends the frame across the LAN    |

# Example Network
+--------------------+
| Computer A         |
| IP: 192.168.1.10   |
| MAC: AA-AA-AA-AA   |
+--------------------+
          |
          |
      Ethernet Switch
          |
          |
+--------------------+
| Computer B         |
| IP: 192.168.1.20   |
| MAC: BB-BB-BB-BB   |
+--------------------+

Computer A knows it wants to send data to 192.168.1.20, but it doesn't know the MAC address.
That's where ARP comes in.

Step 1: Check the ARP Cache

Every operating system keeps a small table of recently learned IP-to-MAC mappings.

Example:

IP Address	MAC Address
192.168.1.1	CC-CC-CC-CC
192.168.1.20	BB-BB-BB-BB

If the address is already here...

✅ No ARP request is needed.

Step 2: Send an ARP Request

If the MAC isn't known, the computer broadcasts a question to everyone.

"Who has IP 192.168.1.20?
Tell 192.168.1.10"

Broadcast destination:

FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF

That MAC address means:

Everyone on this local network, listen.

Step 3: Every Device Receives It
Computer A
      |
      | ARP Request
      V
-------------------------
Switch
-------------------------
 |      |       |
 PC1   PC2    PC3

Every computer checks:

"Is my IP address 192.168.1.20?"

Most say:

No.
Ignore it.

One says:

Yes!
That's me.
Step 4: ARP Reply

Computer B replies directly (unicast):

192.168.1.20 is at

BB-BB-BB-BB

Only Computer A receives this reply.

Step 5: Cache the Result

Computer A stores:

IP	MAC
192.168.1.20	BB-BB-BB-BB

Now it doesn't have to ask again until the entry expires.

Step 6: Send the Data

Now the Ethernet frame can be built.

Ethernet Header

Destination MAC:
BB-BB-BB-BB

Source MAC:
AA-AA-AA-AA

Payload:
IP Packet

The switch delivers it to the correct device.

