Security Concerns

Because ARP has no built-in authentication, attackers on the same local network can abuse it.

One common attack is ARP spoofing (ARP poisoning):

An attacker sends fake ARP replies.
Victims update their ARP cache with the attacker's MAC address.
Traffic intended for another device is sent to the attacker instead.
The attacker can inspect, modify, or forward the traffic (a man-in-the-middle attack).

Common defenses include:

Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) on managed switches.
Static ARP entries for critical systems.
Encrypted protocols such as HTTPS and SSH to protect data even if traffic is intercepted.
Key Points to Remember
ARP maps an IPv4 address to a MAC address.
It is used only on the local network (LAN).
ARP requests are broadcast to all devices on the LAN.
ARP replies are unicast back to the requester.
Devices keep recently learned mappings in an ARP cache to avoid repeated broadcasts.
On remote destinations, your computer ARPs for the default gateway's MAC address, not the remote host's MAC address.
